From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Walsh <contact.me@thejustinwalsh.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 02:18:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ggml: LLAMA_STAGER=1, a staging ring (pinned buffers + memcpy
 threads) for host-to-device copies of PAGEABLE experts

Strata code in this patch: Copyright (c) 2026 Niko1221 and the Strata contributors, MIT License
(LICENSE added in Strata c0107c3; the full notice is engines/patches/llama-upstream-flash/LICENSE.strata). Ported
from src/prefill/prefill.cpp @ d551edf :122-239 (`struct Stager`: a ring of 16 pinned buffers and 2-4 memcpy workers
for unpinned blobs; a buffer is reused only after the DMA that read it, tracked by an event the issuing side records;
claims are taken in order) and its use at :795-870 and :1159-1194 (the next entries' memcpy runs ahead of the DMA).

Why. With the experts UNPINNED (LLAMA_PIN_EXPERTS=0, the deployed setting since 2026-10-05) the layer-major prefill's
weight slots (0029) are filled by cudaMemcpyAsync from the mmapped model file. From pageable memory that call is not
asynchronous: the driver stages it through its own pinned buffer and the calling host thread is blocked for the copy
(~23 ms for a 256 MiB tensor at the ~11 GB/s pageable rate: docs/FLASH-NEXT.md 8 item 6 and 12.6, derived), so the "overlap" 0029 sets up
(the next layer's three expert tensors uploaded while the current layer's 16 graphs run, LLAMA_LAYER_MAJOR_SLOTS=6) has
nothing to overlap with: the gate (bench/flashnext_gate.py pf-lm6-b8k / pf-lm3-b8k, cand0023+0029, n=3 each, ~25.6K-token
prompts) read 433.1 / 443.4 / 434.6 and 434.1 / 433.1 / 438.7 tok/s with and without prefetch (141 of 144 uploads
prefetched), the layer-major batch 17,594 and 17,274 ms. The same arithmetic says an overlapped upload would bring a
8,192-token batch to about 13.8 s (derived, docs/FLASH-NEXT.md 13.3). Whether it can overlap on this card at all (WDDM)
is what the probe in bench/fn_probe/ measures first.

What. ggml/include/ggml-stager.h, ggml/src/ggml-stager.cpp (ggml-base):
- a ring of n_chunks pinned host buffers (the device's host buffer type; 16 x 16 MiB), n_workers memcpy threads that fill
  them in order from the pageable source, ahead of need; one ISSUER thread that waits for a chunk's memcpy and queues the
  DMA on the stager's own stream (a second backend instance), recording one event per ring buffer; a worker overwrites a
  ring buffer only after that event says the DMA that read it has landed. Strata issues from the launching thread and
  waits for the memcpy there; here the thread that launches compute never blocks on a copy. The unit is a chunk of a
  tensor, not an expert blob, so a 256 MiB tensor streams through 256 MiB of ring.
- a request names the event to record after its last chunk and an optional event the stream waits for first (the last
  reader of the destination slot). The launch that reads a staged slot first calls ggml_stager_wait_enqueued(): the
  slot's `ready` event is recorded by the issuer, and a stream wait on an event not yet recorded would wait for nothing.
- OS read-ahead (ggml_stager_will_need: PrefetchVirtualMemory, else madvise WILLNEED, on a thread of its own) for the
  weights of the layer after next, and for layers 0 and 1 when a batch starts: with the experts demand-paged from disk the
  first 8,192-token batch after a load ran at 75-87 s against 16.2-16.9 s warm (bench/results/fn_first_prompt/20261006-a,
  n=3, ~0.4 GB/s); parallel worker copies and the hint turn that into queued reads.
- ggml-backend.cpp: resident slots take the staged path for a weight that is not in the device's own pinned host buffer
  (a pinned one is already asynchronous: the direct path as before); ggml-cuda.cu offers "ggml_backend_bind_thread" as a
  registry proc address (the stager's threads make the device current); the layer-major driver names layers 0, 1 and il + 2.
  LLAMA_LAYER_MAJOR_DEBUG=1 prints one more line per batch (memcpy, ring waits, launching-thread block, hints).
- Switch LLAMA_STAGER (unset or 0: off, and every path is the one 0029 has; the slot upload's order of calls is unchanged),
  LLAMA_STAGER_THREADS (Strata's max(2, min(4, hw / 4))), LLAMA_STAGER_CHUNK_MIB (16), LLAMA_STAGER_RING (16: Strata's
  kRing), LLAMA_STAGER_WILLNEED (1). The chunk and the ring are starting values: a chunk is 1.2 ms of a 14 GB/s link
  (Strata's probe of this card on x8), so a copy call's cost is under 2% of it (derived); the probe sweeps threads, chunk
  and ring.

Not done: the expert cache's refills (0025's queued path, which is not in the shipped series; the series' own refill runs
on a worker thread already, so there is nothing for the stager to hide there). A request API that fits them is here.

Tested (tests/test-stager.cpp, ctest; CPU, no GPU): a fake device whose copies land after a random delay, in order, on one
stream with events; the destination poisoned and guarded before every request, the ring buffer poisoned after its DMA
lands; random requests (0 bytes, chunk +-1, several chunks), 1-4 workers, chunks 4-64 KiB (one not a power of two), rings
of 2-9, up to 12 requests per trial with waits at random points: 200 trials 0 wrong destinations, 71,000 checks per
1,500 trials at three seeds, 0 failed; a request that names a wait event lands nothing until it completes; wait_enqueued
returns with the done event recorded and the done event completes after the last byte; free() drains. Controls that must
fail do: workers that skip the DMA wait are caught in 50 of 50 trials; two mutations of the stager (no stream wait for the
wait event; done recorded before the copies) fail 391 and 1,608 checks; a read before the done event sees poison. The
default hooks run over the CPU backend (no events, ordinary-memory ring). test-layer-major (0029, CPU, 48 runs) passes
unchanged. NOT RUN ON A GPU: `test-stager --device CUDA0` (a pageable source through the pinned ring to the card, read
back) is written and compiled with the CUDA build only. NO speed number exists for this patch.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
